{"reference":"Zephaniah 3","book":"Zephaniah","chapter":3,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter moves from accusation and judgment to restoration. The city’s refusal to accept correction is set alongside the gathering of nations for judgment, then the commands for Zion and Jerusalem to rejoice because judgment is removed, YHWH is present, and those who have been afflicted or driven away are gathered and restored. The identity of the city in verses 1–7 is most often understood as Jerusalem, though that identification and the chapter’s literary shaping remain debated.","verse_refs":["Zephaniah 3:1-7","Zephaniah 3:14-17","Zephaniah 3:18-20","Zephaniah 3:20","Zephaniah 3:8"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zephaniah_003_e8b44b7044af6ff97d389328"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"Images and Difficult Phrases","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The opening indictment uses predatory images: officials are roaring lions and judges are evening wolves, while prophets and priests are accused of betrayal and profanation. These pictures intensify the contrast with YHWH, who makes justice known each morning. Later, the promise of a “pure” or changed speech describes peoples calling on YHWH and serving together. Verse 17 also contains a notable translation question: its central Hebrew expression may describe quietness or silence in love, while the Old Greek supports a reading about renewal in affection.","verse_refs":["Zephaniah 3:11-13","Zephaniah 3:17","Zephaniah 3:3-5","Zephaniah 3:6-7","Zephaniah 3:9"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zephaniah_003_eb62b333b680201ab2294294"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Verse 10","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Verse 10 envisions worshipers, or perhaps a dispersed community, coming from beyond the rivers of Cush with an offering. The syntax and the identity of the group are understood differently across textual witnesses, so the verse should be read with that uncertainty in view.","verse_refs":["Zephaniah 3:10","Zephaniah 3:20"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_zephaniah_003_27064b8ede412b5c5c2a0ed3"],"confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}